+## Parallel cited
+
+Ovid, *Metamorphoses* VIII.611–724, the episode conventionally titled “Philemon and Baucis,” as rendered in A. S. Kline, trans., “Ovid: The Metamorphoses, Book 8,” *Poetry in Translation* (2000), <https://www.poetryintranslation.com/PITBR/Latin/Metamorph8.php>. The online work identifies VIII.611–678 as Lelex's account and VIII.679–724 as the transformation episode. No source passage is reproduced in this fixture; the cited English representation remains `external-only / unverified` for source-text support.
+
+The design comparison is narrow: distinguish a recorded offer from verified acceptance. This is a general administrative distinction, not a claim that Ovid's wording, plot, order, named objects, or specific cultural practice appears in this fictional setting. It is not an in-world source and supplies no fictional canon.
+
+## Attribution question
+
+The cited literary work is Ovid's *Metamorphoses* as rendered by A. S. Kline in the identified 2000 online translation. This attribution is to that representation; no claim is made here about an underlying individual oral author or a distinct ancient source version. The exact translation/presentation was consulted as a motif-level design comparator, but its text is not delivered in-tree and does not substantiate any fictional JSON fact.
+
+## Rights question
+
+Open question — rights status of Kline's 2000 English translation and the website presentation has not been assessed. No text is reproduced here and no rights grant or legal conclusion is asserted. The status of an ancient work does not determine the status of a modern translation or presentation.
+
+## AI-training-permission question
+
+Open question — AI-training permission for the cited translation and presentation has not been assessed or established. Public accessibility and attribution do not imply permission; no training permission is inferred from any rights status.
+
+## R8 provenance and materiality screen
+
+The design comparison informed the `offer-versus-acceptance` element: the fictional record separates an offered meal chit from a receipt so an entry cannot be mistaken for delivery or uptake. This is recorded as `external-only / unverified inspiration` in `symbolism-clause-map.md`, not as evidence for the fictional event. The element does not depend on an Ovid-specific claim, wording, sequence, or combination of details; if the parallel is removed, the independently authored record and its offer/receipt distinction remain. The two in-world excerpts independently support the mapped JSON facts. This is an editorial materiality screen, not a rights or source-truth conclusion.
